sequence: pilot W1 and P2 complete (01:08); writing category W2 (2.3 s) and W3 (8.6 s) complete; W4 stream ended after 32 s with 1,292 characters of reasoning, no finish reason and no answer; W5 failed at once with ConnectionRefusedError (111). Port 8080 had nothing listening afterwards and no llama-server process existed. Cause not established: the user did not report stopping it; no out-of-memory, kernel or GPU error lines were found in the kernel log for 01:08 to 01:11. The server terminal's last lines were not seen and should be read.
runner fault found: v1.0 recorded W4 as status "complete" although the stream ended without a finish reason, and recorded W5 as an ordinary result file, so a resume would have skipped both. The three queued category launchers (Python, reasoning, deceptive) were stopped by PID before they could write more failures (exit code 143, deliberate).
actions: W4.json and W5.json were renamed, not deleted, to W4.failed-20261031-010937-server-lost.json and W5.failed-20261031-010937-server-lost.json (raw record kept). quality_run.py v1.1: a stream that ends without a finish reason is "incomplete"; error, incomplete, aborted_temp and aborted_ram attempts are written as <ID>.failed-<time>-<status>.json (not results, retried on resume); a lost server stops the run with exit code 4. quality_blind.py v1.1 ignores .failed files. Tested against a fake server killed mid-answer: status incomplete, exit code 4, no W2.json left behind. All earlier results (126 files in the six complete runs and the 0.9B pilot files) were checked: none has a missing finish reason.
note: the checksums of quality_run.py and quality_blind.py in MANIFEST-2026-10-31-six-complete-runs.sha256 and in the six-run archive now differ from the files on disk (v1.0 versus v1.1); the archive holds v1.0, which produced all stored results.
